#{[17592186059953 :question/worked-solution "Rearrange the equations so that each left side lists the coefficients of $x,y,z$ in that order:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z&=-1\\\\4y+5z&=0\\\\x+6z&=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMatching the indicated entries gives $a=0$, $b=5$, and $c=-1$."] [17592186060277 :question/worked-solution "Each row of the coefficient matrix lists the coefficients of $x,y,z$ in the corresponding equation. The missing entries are the coefficient $5$ of $z$ in the first equation, the coefficient $8$ of $z$ in the second equation, and the coefficient $0$ of $x$ in the third equation. Thus $a=5$, $b=8$, and $c=0$."] [17592186060278 :question/worked-solution "The matrix is diagonal, so its determinant is the product of its diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot7\\cdot(-3)=-42.\n$$"] [17592186060451 :question/worked-solution "The first equation is $1x+0y+0z=6$. The third equation rearranges to $-2x+0y+3z=2$. Matching these coefficients and constants with the matrix representation gives $a=1$, $b=0$, and $c=2$."] [17592186060455 :question/worked-solution "Expanding across the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0&-1&3\\\\2&0&1\\\\7&-1&0\\end{vmatrix}&=0-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2&1\\\\7&0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\\\7&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=(-7)+3(-2)\\\\&=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186088366 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying $A$ and $C$ in both orders gives\n\n$$\nAC=CA=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}=I_2.\n$$\n\nThus $C=A^{-1}$. Since $B\\ne C$, statement III is false. Also\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1&1\\\\-2&0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne I_2,\n$$\n\nso statement I is false. Only statement II is true."] [17592186092817 :question/worked-solution "The constant vector is $b=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\\\-1\\\\-5\\end{bmatrix}$. Multiplying by the given inverse gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x\\\\y\\\\z\\end{bmatrix}=A^{-1}b=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&2\\\\2&-2&3\\\\1&-1&1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\\\-1\\\\-5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\\\1\\\\3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $x+y+z=-3+1+3=1$."] [17592186101102 :question/worked-solution "Here, $X$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. The matching inner dimensions show that the product is defined, and $XY$ is $2\\times 3$:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2\\times\\boxed{{\\color{#2E8B57}{2}}}}{X}\\underset{{\\color{#2E8B57}{\\checkmark}}}{\\cdot}\\underset{\\boxed{{\\color{#2E8B57}{2}}}\\times3}{Y}=\\underset{2\\times3}{XY}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{11}$, use row $1$ of $X$ (blue) and column $1$ of $Y$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}{1}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\\boxed{0} & \\ast & \\ast \\\\ \\ast & \\ast & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{11}&={\\color{#4169E1}{1}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{3}}+{\\color{#4169E1}{3}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-1}}) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{12}$, use row $1$ of $X$ (blue) and column $2$ of $Y$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}{1}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{3}} & {\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{-1}} & {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\boxed{1} & \\ast \\\\ \\ast & \\ast & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{12}&={\\color{#4169E1}{1}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}+{\\color{#4169E1}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{13}$, use row $1$ of $X$ (blue) and column $3$ of $Y$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}{1}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{3}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{-1}} & {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & \\boxed{14} \\\\ \\ast & \\ast & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{13}&={\\color{#4169E1}{1}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}}+{\\color{#4169E1}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ &=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{21}$, use row $2$ of $X$ (blue) and column $1$ of $Y$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}{0}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ \\boxed{1} & \\ast & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{21}&={\\color{#4169E1}{0}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{3}}+({\\color{#4169E1}{-1}})\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-1}}) \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{22}$, use row $2$ of $X$ (blue) and column $2$ of $Y$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}{0}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{3}} & {\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{-1}} & {\\color{red}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 1 & \\boxed{0} & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{22}&={\\color{#4169E1}{0}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}+({\\color{#4169E1}{-1}})\\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{23}$, use row $2$ of $X$ (blue) and column $3$ of $Y$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}{0}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{3}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{-1}} & {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{red}{4}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\boxed{-4}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{23}&={\\color{#4169E1}{0}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}}+({\\color{#4169E1}{-1}})\\cdot {\\color{red}{4}} \\\\ &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nXY=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186101103 :question/worked-solution "The product of a $3\\times1$ matrix and a $1\\times2$ matrix is a $3\\times2$ matrix. Multiply each entry of the column by each entry of the row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2&4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot2&3\\cdot4\\\\0\\cdot2&0\\cdot4\\\\1\\cdot2&1\\cdot4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6&12\\\\0&0\\\\2&4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186101104 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. The matching inner dimensions show that the product is defined, and $AB$ is $2\\times 2$:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2\\times\\boxed{{\\color{#2E8B57}{3}}}}{A}\\underset{{\\color{#2E8B57}{\\checkmark}}}{\\cdot}\\underset{\\boxed{{\\color{#2E8B57}{3}}}\\times2}{B}=\\underset{2\\times2}{AB}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{11}$, use row $1$ of $A$ (blue) and column $1$ of $B$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}{5}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{-2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{6}} & {\\color{gray}{5}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{9}} & {\\color{gray}{8}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{gray}{7}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\\boxed{48} & \\ast \\\\ \\ast & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{11}&={\\color{#4169E1}{5}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{6}}+{\\color{#4169E1}{1}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{9}} \\\\ &\\quad +{\\color{#4169E1}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ &=48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{12}$, use row $1$ of $A$ (blue) and column $2$ of $B$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}{5}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{0}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{-2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{6}} & {\\color{red}{5}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{9}} & {\\color{red}{8}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{3}} & {\\color{red}{7}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}48 & \\boxed{54} \\\\ \\ast & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{12}&={\\color{#4169E1}{5}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{5}}+{\\color{#4169E1}{1}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{8}} \\\\ &\\quad +{\\color{#4169E1}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{7}} \\\\ &=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{21}$, use row $2$ of $A$ (blue) and column $1$ of $B$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{5}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}{0}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{-2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{6}} & {\\color{gray}{5}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{9}} & {\\color{gray}{8}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{3}} & {\\color{gray}{7}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}48 & 54 \\\\ \\boxed{3} & \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{21}&={\\color{#4169E1}{0}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{6}}+{\\color{#4169E1}{1}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{9}} \\\\ &\\quad +({\\color{#4169E1}{-2}})\\cdot {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor entry $c_{22}$, use row $2$ of $A$ (blue) and column $2$ of $B$ (red):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{5}} & {\\color{gray}{1}} & {\\color{gray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}{0}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} & {\\color{#4169E1}{-2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{6}} & {\\color{red}{5}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{9}} & {\\color{red}{8}} \\\\ {\\color{gray}{3}} & {\\color{red}{7}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}48 & 54 \\\\ 3 & \\boxed{-6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{22}&={\\color{#4169E1}{0}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{5}}+{\\color{#4169E1}{1}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{8}} \\\\ &\\quad +({\\color{#4169E1}{-2}})\\cdot {\\color{red}{7}} \\\\ &=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}48 & 54 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186101140 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $C$ has size $1\\times2$, while $D$ has size $3\\times1$. The number of columns in $C$ is $2$, and the number of rows in $D$ is $3$. These inner dimensions differ, so $CD$ is undefined."] [17592186101529 :question/worked-solution "Multiply row $1$ of $A$ by column $3$ of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c_{13}&=\\begin{bmatrix}-1&2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\\\5\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=(-1)(-2)+2(5)\\\\&=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186101530 :question/worked-solution "Multiply row $4$ of $X$ by column $2$ of $Y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z_{42}&=\\begin{bmatrix}-5&6&-2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\\\0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=(-5)(-3)+6(0)+(-2)(1)\\\\&=13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106290 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1&0&2&2\\\\0&1&-3&1\\\\2&0&-4&-4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nApply $R_1:=-R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3-2R_1$. This gives the row echelon form\n\n$$\nM\\sim\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&0&-2&-2\\\\0&1&-3&1\\\\0&0&0&0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$"] [17592186115742 :question/worked-solution "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}$. Then\n\n$$\nEA=\\begin{bmatrix}1&-2\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a-2c&b-2d\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe second row is unchanged, and the first row becomes the first row minus twice the second row. Therefore the operation is $R_1:=R_1+(-2)R_2$."] [17592186115751 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&1&2&19\\\\3&1&2&31\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nApply $R_2:=R_2-3R_1$, then $R_2:=-\\frac12R_2$, and finally $R_1:=R_1-R_2$. The reduced row echelon form is\n\n$$\nM\\sim\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&0&0&6\\\\0&1&2&13\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nHence $x=6$ and $y=13-2z$, with $z$ free. The solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6\\\\13-2z\\\\z\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$"] [17592186115844 :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_1:=R_1-5R_2$ corresponds to left multiplication by $E_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1&-5\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$. The operation $R_1:=3R_1$ corresponds to left multiplication by $E_2=\\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$. Because $E_1$ acts first, the required product is\n\n$$\nE_2E_1A=\\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1&-5\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}A.\n$$"] [17592186299543 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-4y+6z &=-1 \\\\ -2x+3y &=0 \\\\ x-9y+z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$-4$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$1$</td><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$5$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186299544 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=14 \\\\ 3x+3y+z &=3 \\\\ 5x-8y+4z &=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$3$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>$-8$</td><td>$4$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$14$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-7$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186299545 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x &=6 \\\\ 2x+z &=2 \\\\ 3x+4y-2z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$2$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$x$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$y$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$z$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$6$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">.</span>\n</div>"] [17592186299546 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y-z &=1 \\\\ y-3z &=0 \\\\ 4z &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$3$</td><td>$2$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$1$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$0$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$4$<